issue_context.body:
This report came into the security mailing list.  Since it is an internal header that is currently unused in any released version of OpenSSL and there is no demonstration of a path where an attacker could actually provide spurious values, this isn't a CVE and will be treated as a potential bug.

> In OpenSSL there is a suspected vulnerability for a potential overflow, due to a lack of checking for bounds for unsigned integers for size_t. This is for the buffer ring ring_buf.h under include/internal. for size_t alloc, there is a check for size_t for buflen to assure there is no negative value, but there is still no bound checking for max int 4294967295 for the size_t of buff len.
> page: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/master/include/internal/ring_buf.h
> here is conceptual PoC where a user can try and input some size_t integer that can cause overflow due to lack of unsigned checks:
> ```c
> include <stdio.h>
> include <stdlib.h>
> include <pthread.h>
> include <unistd.h> // For sleep
> include "ring_buf.h"
> define BUFFER_SIZE 1024
> define NUM_PRODUCERS 3
> define NUM_CONSUMERS 2
> define DATA_TO_PRODUCE "Hello from producer %d!\n"
> struct ThreadData {
>     int thread_id;
>     struct ring_buf *buffer;
> };
> void producer_thread(void arg) {
>     struct ThreadData data = (struct ThreadData )arg;
>     int producer_id = data->thread_id;
> 
>     for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
>         char data_to_write[50];
>         snprintf(data_to_write, sizeof(data_to_write), DATA_TO_PRODUCE, producer_id);
>         size_t data_len = strlen(data_to_write);
> 
>         ring_buf_write_at(data->buffer, ring_buf_used(data->buffer), (const unsigned char *)data_to_write, data_len);
> 
>         printf("Producer %d wrote: %s", producer_id, data_to_write);
>         sleep(1);
>     }
>     pthread_exit(NULL);
> ```
> To assure if there was any extra checks on other imports of the openssl library such as openssl/e_os2.h, I checked to see if there were any checks outsourced from other scripts, which didn't seem to be found, besides some other checks,
> ```c
> # if defined(STDC_VERSION) && STDC_VERSION >= 199901L && \
>       defined(INTMAX_MAX) && defined(UINTMAX_MAX)
> typedef intmax_t ossl_intmax_t;
> typedef uintmax_t ossl_uintmax_t;
> ```
> though this isn't related to any checks for unsigned integers overflow, although the code for buffer ring does check for underflow. The goal for an attacker would be to indirectly get the buffer ring to get a large unsigned value of size_t which a server can't take in, which in turn would lead to lack of bounds and a form of integer "overflow" due to lack of checking. I use overflow lightly as it's not like a general overflow as that would be a wrap around for unsigned integers, but large values before such wrapping, due to lack of integrity checking.
> Impact
> An attacker could utilize an integer overflow by interacting indirectly with some large size_t. Which due to a lack of bounds, could case undefined behaviour, which may or may not include buffer overflow. Although there is a wrap around for unsigned integers, they still expand to large unsigned integers and the lack of some bounds for incredibly large integers. But interactions with buffer ring wouldn't be straightforwardly interacted with compared to in comparison to that of regular buffers.
